 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What a preposition does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "A preposition is a small word in front of a noun that says where or how: \"to the house\", \"through the garden\". After these five, the Latin noun is always in the <b>accusative</b>."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"c\">ad</u> <u class=\"v\">villam</u> ambulat.",
      "en": "The slave walks <b>to the house</b>.",
      "note": "<em>ad</em> takes the accusative: <em>villam</em>, not <em>villa</em>."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The preposition and its noun make a phrase. Translate them together.",
      "An accusative after a preposition is not the object of the verb. It is simply the case the preposition takes.",
      "Most of these five describe movement: going to, into, through or around something."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "list",
   "h2": "The five prepositions",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "English",
       "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>ad</em>",
        "to, towards",
        "<em>ad villam</em>: to the house"
       ],
       [
        "<em>in</em>",
        "into, onto",
        "<em>in hortum</em>: into the garden"
       ],
       [
        "<em>per</em>",
        "through, along",
        "<em>per insulam</em>: through the island"
       ],
       [
        "<em>circum</em>",
        "around",
        "<em>circum villam</em>: around the house"
       ],
       [
        "<em>contra</em>",
        "against",
        "<em>contra dominum</em>: against the master"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "<em>in</em> has a second meaning, \"in\" or \"on\", with a different case. That is in Chapter 2. For now, <em>in</em> with the accusative means movement: \"into\" or \"onto\"."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating the phrase",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the preposition, then the noun after it in the accusative.",
      "Keep the pair together in English: \"into the garden\".",
      "Then the rest of the sentence as usual: subject, verb, object."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi <u class=\"c\">contra</u> <u class=\"v\">dominum</u> pugnant.",
      "en": "The slaves fight <b>against the master</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "puellae <u class=\"c\">in</u> <u class=\"v\">hortum</u> ambulant.",
      "en": "The girls walk <b>into the garden</b>.",
      "note": "<em>in</em> with the accusative is \"into\", not \"in\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nuntius epistulam <u class=\"c\">ad</u> <u class=\"v\">dominum</u> portat.",
      "en": "The messenger carries the letter <b>to the master</b>.",
      "note": "Two accusatives with two jobs: <em>epistulam</em> is the object, and <em>dominum</em> is accusative because of <em>ad</em>."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>in</em> with the accusative is \"into\" or \"onto\", not \"in\".",
      "A noun straight after a preposition belongs to the preposition, not to the verb.",
      "<em>circum</em> is \"around\" and <em>contra</em> is \"against\": they are easy to mix up."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
